::ALG2_U04_L05_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_4(ab).
{
=A. log_4(a) + log_4(b)#Correct. Use the product property.
~B. log_4(a) - log_4(b)#This is the quotient pattern.
~C. log_4(a + b)#This changes multiplication to addition inside one log.
~D. log_4(a/b)#This is not equivalent to a product.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_3(x/y).
{
~A. log_3(x) + log_3(y)#Addition corresponds to multiplication.
=B. log_3(x) - log_3(y)#Correct. Use the quotient property.
~C. log_3(x - y)#There is no property for subtraction inside the argument.
~D. log_3(y) - log_3(x)#This reverses the quotient.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_6(x⁵).
{
~A. log_6(5x)#The exponent becomes a coefficient, not a factor.
~B. log_6(x) + 5#This is not the power property.
=C. 5log_6(x)#Correct. Use the power property.
~D. log_6(x)/5#This does not match exponent behavior.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense log_2(m) + log_2(n).
{
~A. log_2(m/n)#Subtraction would make a quotient.
~B. log_2(m + n)#There is no sum property inside the argument.
~C. log_2(m - n)#This is not equivalent.
=D. log_2(mn)#Correct. Same-base logarithms added condense to a product.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense log_8(p) - log_8(q).
{
=A. log_8(p/q)#Correct. Same-base logarithms subtracted condense to a quotient.
~B. log_8(pq)#This would match addition.
~C. log_8(p - q)#There is no subtraction property inside the argument.
~D. log_8(q/p)#This reverses the quotient.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense 2log_5(x).
{
~A. log_5(2x)#This turns the coefficient into a factor incorrectly.
=B. log_5(x²)#Correct. The coefficient becomes an exponent.
~C. log_5(x + 2)#This is not a logarithmic property.
~D. 2 + log_5(x)#This changes multiplication into addition.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which property is used in log_b(MN) = log_b(M) + log_b(N)?
{
~A. Quotient property#The quotient property involves subtraction.
~B. Power property#The power property moves exponents.
=C. Product property#Correct. A product inside a log becomes a sum of logs.
~D. Change of base#Change of base rewrites the base using a ratio of logs.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which property is used in log_b(M/N) = log_b(M) - log_b(N)?
{
~A. Product property#The product property involves addition.
~B. Power property#The power property moves exponents.
~C. Change of base#Change of base rewrites the base.
=D. Quotient property#Correct. A quotient inside a log becomes a difference of logs.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which property is used in log_b(Mᵏ) = klog_b(M)?
{
=A. Power property#Correct. The exponent becomes a coefficient.
~B. Product property#The product property splits multiplication.
~C. Quotient property#The quotient property splits division.
~D. Change of base#Change of base changes the logarithm base.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Rewrite log_3(10) using common logarithms.
{
~A. log(3)/log(10)#This reverses input and base.
=B. log(10)/log(3)#Correct. Change of base gives log(input)/log(base).
~C. log(30)#This multiplies inside one log incorrectly.
~D. log(10 - 3)#This is not change of base.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_2(3x⁴).
{
~A. log_2(3) + log_2(4x)#The exponent 4 should become a coefficient.
~B. 4log_2(3x)#The exponent applies only to x, not to 3x.
=C. log_2(3) + 4log_2(x)#Correct. Use product and power properties.
~D. log_2(3 + x⁴)#This changes multiplication to addition inside the argument.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense log_4(a) + 3log_4(b).
{
~A. log_4(3ab)#The coefficient becomes an exponent, not a factor.
~B. log_4(a + b³)#Addition of logs becomes multiplication, not addition inside one log.
~C. log_4(a/b³)#Subtraction would create a quotient.
=D. log_4(ab³)#Correct. Move 3 to an exponent, then use the product property.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense 2log_7(x) - log_7(y).
{
=A. log_7(x²/y)#Correct. Use the power property, then the quotient property.
~B. log_7(2x/y)#The coefficient 2 should become an exponent.
~C. log_7(x²y)#Subtraction creates division, not multiplication.
~D. log_7(y/x²)#This reverses the quotient.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to log_5(x²y)?
{
~A. log_5(2x) + log_5(y)#The exponent 2 becomes a coefficient, not a factor.
=B. 2log_5(x) + log_5(y)#Correct. Use power and product properties.
~C. 2log_5(xy)#The exponent applies only to x.
~D. log_5(x²) - log_5(y)#This would represent division by y.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to log_9(m/n³)?
{
~A. log_9(m) + 3log_9(n)#This represents multiplication by n cubed.
~B. 3log_9(m) - log_9(n)#The exponent 3 applies to n, not m.
=C. log_9(m) - 3log_9(n)#Correct. Use quotient and power properties.
~D. log_9(m - n³)#Subtraction inside a log is not equivalent.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which rewrite is valid?
{
~A. log_2(x + y) = log_2(x) + log_2(y)#There is no sum property.
~B. log_2(x - y) = log_2(x) - log_2(y)#There is no subtraction-inside property.
~C. log_2(x/y) = log_2(y) - log_2(x)#This reverses the quotient.
=D. log_2(xy) = log_2(x) + log_2(y)#Correct. This is the product property.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which rewrite is invalid?
{
=A. log_3(a + b) = log_3(a) + log_3(b)#Correct. There is no logarithm property for splitting addition inside the argument.
~B. log_3(ab) = log_3(a) + log_3(b)#This is valid by the product property.
~C. log_3(a/b) = log_3(a) - log_3(b)#This is valid by the quotient property.
~D. log_3(a²) = 2log_3(a)#This is valid by the power property.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Rewrite log_4(17) using natural logarithms.
{
~A. ln(4)/ln(17)#This reverses input and base.
=B. ln(17)/ln(4)#Correct. Change of base can use any consistent log base.
~C. ln(68)#This multiplies input and base incorrectly.
~D. ln(17 - 4)#This is not change of base.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense log_2(x) + log_2(y) - log_2(z).
{
~A. log_2(x + y - z)#Log properties do not preserve addition or subtraction inside the argument.
~B. log_2(xz/y)#This puts the quotient in the wrong order.
=C. log_2(xy/z)#Correct. Addition gives multiplication and subtraction gives division.
~D. log_2(z/xy)#This reverses the full quotient.
}


::ALG2_U04_L05_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_6((a²b)/c).
{
~A. log_6(2a) + log_6(b) - log_6(c)#The exponent should become a coefficient.
~B. 2log_6(ab) - log_6(c)#The exponent applies only to a.
~C. 2log_6(a) - log_6(bc)#This places b in the denominator incorrectly.
=D. 2log_6(a) + log_6(b) - log_6(c)#Correct. Use power, product, and quotient properties.
}

